    "title": "Frontend Roadmap for 50 LPA",
    "description": "## TL;DR\n- A 50 LPA frontend engineer must master JavaScript, React, and system-level concepts like performance and security.\n- The path is structured in three levels: foundation, intermediate, and expert.\n- Real-world projects and deep understanding of \"why\" are critical for success.\n\n## What it is\nThe \"Frontend Roadmap for 50 LPA\" is a structured learning path that outlines the skills and knowledge required to become a senior frontend developer earning 50 LPA (Lakhs Per Annum) in India. It breaks down the journey into three levels, each building on the previous, and emphasizes the importance of depth over breadth.\n\n## How it works\n1. **Level 1 – Basics**: Focus on HTML, CSS, JavaScript fundamentals, and tooling. This is the foundation for all future learning.\n2. **Level 2 – Intermediate**: Dive deeper into JavaScript, React, state management, and networking. This level differentiates between average and advanced developers.\n3. **Level 3 – Expert**: Master rendering, performance, build tools, security, and system design. This level is for problem solvers who understand trade-offs and architecture.\n\n## What you can do with it\n- **Realistic**: Build a strong foundation in frontend development, master React and modern JavaScript, and become a capable full-stack developer.\n- **Speculative**: Design scalable frontend systems, lead teams, and contribute to architectural decisions at the enterprise level.\n\n## Key details & nuances\n- **Level 1 is non-negotiable**: Skipping it leads to weak foundations and difficulty in grasping advanced concepts.\n- **Depth > breadth**: Understanding a few concepts deeply is more valuable than knowing many superficially.\n- **Real projects matter**: Building working examples is essential for interview readiness and portfolio strength.\n- **\"Why\" is more important than \"what\"**: Interviewers look for understanding of principles, not just syntax.\n\n## Notable examples / tools\n- **Tools**: Git, npm, Tailwind CSS, React Query, React Testing Library, Playwright.\n- **Frameworks**: React, Next.js, GraphQL, WebSockets.\n- **Concepts**: SSR, hydration, tree shaking, code splitting, performance budgets.\n\n## How to go deeper\n1. Build a simple portfolio site using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.\n2. Create a React app with state management using Redux or Zustand.\n3. Implement a REST API client with Axios and handle error states.\n4. Explore React Query for managing server state and caching.\n5. Study performance metrics like LCP, CLS, and INP and implement improvements.\n\n## Open questions\n- How can one effectively balance depth and breadth in frontend development?\n- What are the best practices for teaching \"why\" in frontend interviews?\n- How can developers measure and improve frontend performance systematically?\n- What are the most common pitfalls in transitioning from intermediate to expert-level development?\n- How can a developer build a strong portfolio without real-world experience?",
